WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.740
Welcome to fascinating frontiers episode 69.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.060
Today is May 12th, 2026.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.460
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:14.820
A supernova cop mid explosion in a spiral galaxy

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.740
offers a rare window into stellar death

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:19.940
at 50 million light years away.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.180
An amateur astronomer stacked more than two hours of images

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.160
to capture SN2026 kid exploding inside the edge

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.500
on spiral galaxy NGC5907.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.920
The detection marks the observer's first supernova find

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.980
and shows that even modest backyard setups

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.180
can record genuine transient events in real time.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.780
At a distance of 50 million light years,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.940
the explosion gives astronomers an unusually clear look

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.540
at the final moments of a massive star.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.740
Such observations help refine models of stellar

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.760
evolution and the triggers that lead to core collapse.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.880
The result also highlights how accessible equipment

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.260
continues to fill gaps left by professional surveys

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:12.100
that cannot monitor every galaxy continuously.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.420
On a different note, a new high-resolution portrait

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.900
of the pinwheel galaxy M101 has been released

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.500
using ground-based gear.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:28.360
The image combines 4,173 seconds of exposures

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.340
taken across multiple states with a small refractor

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.100
and an unmodified DSLR camera.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.760
Spiral arms and active star-forming regions

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.280
stand out clearly even though the data

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.260
came from sites chosen to limit light pollution

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:46.120
in the American Southwest.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.840
This approach demonstrates that careful technique

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.740
and longer total integration times can produce

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.460
professional grade deep-sky results

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.640
without specialized observatories.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.520
Observers gain fresh detail on how star formation propagates

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.200
along the galaxy's arms,

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.080
information that complements data from larger facilities.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.900
The work underscores how advances in consumer equipment

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.400
and processing methods keep expanding

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.720
what dedicated amateurs can contribute.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.800
Next up, an abandoned Falcon 9 upper stage

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.820
has created a fresh crater on the lunar surface

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:27.380
after an earlier mission.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.980
The second stage struck the moon at high speed

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.200
and orbiting spacecraft are now examining

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.940
the resulting impact site.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.580
This unplanned event supplies direct data

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:43.000
on how rocket hardware behaves during lunar collisions

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.540
and how ejecta patterns form in the low-gravity environment.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.760
Studying the crater helps calibrate models

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.920
used to interpret other impact features across the moon

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:55.220
and similar bodies.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.360
The observation arrives at a useful time

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.860
as mission planners prepare for more frequent landings

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:02.900
in the coming decade.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:05.820
After that accidental experiment,

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.660
attention turns to deliberate hardware testing

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.660
for the Exel Mars 2028 mission.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.360
Engineers at Thales and Nea Space

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.580
have completed full-scale structural test models

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.360
of both the rover and its carrier spacecraft.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:24.120
These replicas will undergo vibration, thermal,

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.660
and load testing to verify designs

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.360
before any flight hardware is placed at risk.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.280
Completing the models keeps the overall schedule aligned

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.200
with the plan launch window in 2028.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:40.660
The process illustrates the careful qualification steps

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:43.140
required for interplanetary missions

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.100
where repairs remain impossible.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.300
From European preparations for Mars,

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.480
the focus shifts to NASA's longer-term plans

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.760
for sustained operations on the moon.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.220
The Artemis program is developing reusable landers,

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.000
power systems, and habitat modules

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.900
to support crews staying for months at a time.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.940
Planners emphasize incremental growth,

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.440
building experience step-by-step

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:13.280
rather than attempting permanent bases immediately.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.120
This architecture aims to create reliable logistics

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.460
and living systems that can expand

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:21.960
as capabilities mature.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:26.060
The approach reflects lessons from earlier programs

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.880
about the value of repeated surface operations

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:30.460
before scaling further.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.000
Engineers are also refining the next generation

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.360
of spacesuits intended for those extended lunar stays

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.340
and deeper space activities.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.880
The design's prioritized improved mobility,

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:46.640
better dust resistance, and life support backpacks

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.980
suited to longer exploration timelines.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:53.440
Suits must function reliably both on the lunar surface

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:57.440
and during transit phases where conditions differ significantly.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:01.120
Iterative testing continues to balance protection

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.520
with the flexibility astronauts need

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.220
for practical tasks outside habitats.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.420
On a related note, the Artemis II crew

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:13.040
has already demonstrated how liquids behave

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.240
without gravity during their flight.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:19.200
Footage shows astronauts releasing a single droplet

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.160
of water that formed a near-perfect sphere

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.560
held together only by surface tension.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:29.320
The crew could poke, stretch, and split the droplet

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:32.440
with surprising control, revealing behavior

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:34.340
that gravity masks on Earth.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.560
These observations feed directly into the design

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.620
of fuel tanks, water recycling systems,

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.220
and medical equipment for future missions.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.480
Engineers are already applying the insights

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.640
to refine droplet management inside closed-loop life support

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:52.200
hardware.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:56.840
What remains less certain is how prolonged radiation exposure

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:00.080
or cabin materials might gradually change

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.940
those same surface tension properties over months

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.120
in deep space.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.440
You know what is fascinating about this?

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.780
Picture the International Space Station

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.640
as a sealed room, the size of a large house,

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:16.180
yet every loose droplet inside behaves as though

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:18.020
it were the only thing that matters.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.640
On Earth, a milliliter of water collapses

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:23.960
under its own weight into a puddle.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:27.520
In orbit, the same volume is pulled inward

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:29.980
from all directions by surface tension

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:32.780
until it becomes a near-perfect sphere,

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:34.880
roughly the diameter of a grape.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:39.000
That sphere can grow or shrink simply by adding

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.160
or removing tiny amounts of liquid

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.220
because nothing is pressing it against the surface.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:48.220
Astronauts have measured these droplets reaching several

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:51.240
centimeters across before they merge or split,

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:56.340
all while floating at speeds matching the station's 28,000

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:58.820
kilometers per hour around the planet.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.480
The same physics governs how fuel settles inside rocket tanks

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:07.900
and how sweat behaves on an astronaut's skin during a space walk.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.840
Yet researchers still cannot fully predict

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:14.440
how trace contaminants or prolonged radiation exposure

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.280
will change the surface tension of these droplets

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.760
over the course of a six-month mission.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:22.320
From in-flight fluid experiments,

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:25.240
the conversation moves to university teams

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.100
contributing hardware and analysis for upcoming flights.

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:33.080
Faculty and students at Arizona State University

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:36.400
are supplying instruments and data analysis pipelines

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.300
to several NASA and commercial spacecraft

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:40.340
now in development.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:43.860
Their work centers on surface chemistry measurements,

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.940
radiation monitoring, and autonomous navigation systems

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.040
that will operate on future missions.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:54.520
These partnerships spend both government and private vehicles

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:58.760
giving students direct experience with flight-qualified technology.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:02.980
The involvement strengthens ties between academic research

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:05.200
and operational space programs.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:09.080
Finally, a third-graders artwork has been selected

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:12.160
to fly aboard an upcoming SpaceX cargo mission

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.000
to the International Space Station.

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:18.920
The students drawing from Apollo Beach, Florida

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:21.540
will travel alongside experiments and supplies

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:24.180
already scheduled for the resupply flight.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:27.260
Such selections continue a long tradition

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:30.180
of educational outreach that lets young people

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:32.520
see their creations reach orbit.

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:36.620
The gesture reinforces how spaceflight programs

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:38.720
maintain connections with the public

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:41.040
even as technical complexity grows.

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:44.380
New hardware and observations keep revealing

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:46.300
how little we still understand

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:48.500
about operating far from Earth.

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:52.660
Keep an eye on upcoming tests of surface chemistry instruments

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:55.160
from university teams that could reshape

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:59.480
how we interpret data from the next Mars and lunar landings.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.060
That covers today's space and science news.

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:06.180
Share this with a fellow space enthusiast

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:07.460
if you found it interesting.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:09.800
I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:11.100
See you tomorrow.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:15.720
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice,

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:19.080
editorial selection, and analysis are my own.
